# {{ .Name }}

A cafaye app, scaffolded by `caf new --template app`. It runs on `caf dev`,
ships from a Helm chart `caf gen chart` writes, and is held to the cafaye
contract by `caf contract lint`.

## The loop

```sh
caf dev              # build the image and run the stack locally
caf gen chart        # the Helm chart this app deploys from
caf contract lint .  # the manifest, checked against core's schema
```

## What is in the box

| Path | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `cafaye.yml` | the app's identity: name, language, contract version, owner, repository |
| `main.go` | a real service on `:8080`, answering `/healthz` and `/readyz` |
| `go.mod` | the Go module |
| `docker/Dockerfile` | the image `caf dev` builds, in the place it looks for one |

The app listens on 8080 because that is the port cafaye knows a Go service
listens on — it is read out of the manifest's `language`, not configured
anywhere, so the manifest and the container cannot disagree.

## The two rules that matter most

**The manifest is identity, not configuration.** Core's schema is
`additionalProperties: false`: a field core has not declared is an error, and
`caf contract lint` reports it. A credential belongs in the deployment's
secret store, referenced by name — never in a file that is committed. When
this app starts publishing events or serving an API, declare them in
`exposes`: every event type opens with the publisher's own name, and
`caf contract lint` enforces that too.

**The app is the editable surface.** The customer's repository is the source
of truth; the platform's surface is its contracts and configuration. An agent
that patches vendored platform service source creates a fork that has to be
supported through every update. Extend this app through the contract
(`cafaye.yml`, the OpenAPI document, the event types), not by editing the
platform underneath it.
